    "narrative": "For Ghost Ride the WHIP on this May 17th slate, we are leaning heavily into speed and favorable park factors to navigate a day devoid of elite aces. **Corbin Carroll** (85.94) is our clear daily anchor; facing Lorenzen in a hitter-friendly environment, his elite sprint speed and top-of-the-order positioning make him an auto-start. He is complemented by **James Wood** (76.28), whose leadoff spot and strong xwOBA against Young provide a high floor, and **Mickey Moniak** (73.50), who benefits from a massive 14% park boost and the dynamic platoon advantage against Soroka. While Soroka is a veteran, his current profile offers little resistance to the speed-focused profile of our outfield, making this trio the primary engine for our SB and R production today.\n\nThe most difficult bench decision involved **Kyle Karros** (44.88), who narrowly missed the cut behind the more efficient profile of **Cole Young** (48.10). While **Kyle Karros** benefits from the same favorable park conditions as **Mickey Moniak**, the raw efficiency of the remaining infield core, particularly the SP-skill matchup for **Vinnie Pasquantino** (72.92) and **Josh Naylor** (71.03) against the vulnerable Giolito, made keeping **Kyle Karros** on the bench the mathematically sound move. **Brice Turang** (70.33) remains a priority start despite a slight wind-in penalty, as his high-percentage SB environment against a slow-moving Ober is too valuable to sacrifice. \n\nWe are monitoring the wind closely, specifically for **Brice Turang**, where the 5% penalty could dampen power potential; however, the stolen base upside effectively hedges that risk. We are also keeping a watchful eye on our IL depth, as **Jose Caballero** and **Trevor Story** remain sidelined, limiting our flexibility should any late lineup scratches occur. While we await official word on the bottom-third of the order, we are confident that the combined projection of our active lineup maintains our competitive edge in the scarcest categories, assuming no sudden weather volatility in the mid-afternoon games.",
